68 #edb/ref 17592186411675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204239 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204239 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204239 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A=R,$ and consider the relation \"greater than or equal to\" on $A.$ Express the relation using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\nR={{{field-1}}\\in {{field-2}}\\,:\\,{{field-3}}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204239 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e3a6733-3b0e-5ea6-a342-f677785c09f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204239 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nFor a relation $R$ on a set $A,$ we also have the following notations:\n\n- $a\\,R\\,b$ if and only if $(a,b)\\in R$\n\n- $a\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,b$ if and only if $(a,b)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}$ such that $x\\ge y.$\n\nTherefore, we can write out $R$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge y}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204623 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204623 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204623 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204623 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the relation $R$ on $Z$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣(y-1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $4\\,R\\,13$\n\nII. $3\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,11$\n\nIII. $8\\,R\\,3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204623 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e6ad6a4-a09f-5fba-92c0-ff271a2f98be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204623 #edb/ref 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣(y-1).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $4∣(13-1),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $3∤(11-1),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, we have $8∣(3-1),$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204654 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204654 #edb/ref 1061 152220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204654 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b79cebe-4264-5034-b9e7-c2090474f283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204654 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204655 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204655 #edb/ref 1061 152221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204655 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3bcc0ee7-5449-5f3a-918c-6f17ebe72a76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204655 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204656 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204656 #edb/ref 1061 152224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204656 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88558eba-a376-56f6-a415-b88b7184c760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204656 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204657 #edb/ref 1061 152227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d73d61de-6b85-5431-b62e-d2e07d4cb05b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205236 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a binary relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2{:}$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we have the following notations:\n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\in R$\n\n- $\\require{cancel}x \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\notin R$\n\nRelations can be defined over infinite sets. For example, consider the relation $R$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by\n\n$$\nx \\: R \\: y \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad x \\geq y.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x \\geq y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\geq$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ and $\\not\\geq$ (or, equivalently, $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ and $\\lt$) are also interchangeable.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $2 \\: R \\: 0$ since $2 \\geq 0$\n\n- $\\require{cancel} 1 \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: \\pi$ since $1 \\not\\geq \\pi$\n\n- $0 \\: R \\: 0$ since $0 \\geq 0$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2$ such that $x \\geq y,$ we can write out $R$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2 \\: : \\: x \\geq y \\big\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205236 #edb/ref 1065 9060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205236 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f63d42cd-e795-5986-8ce3-11dc8e48fcaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205236 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411621 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411621 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411621 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04b7cd61-6d77-5fba-b3b8-52a35ad4aff7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411622 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411622 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411622 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08f7679f-73f5-5a0e-a03f-dee0f63f3f77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411623 #edb/ref 1080 "{R}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411623 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411623 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e6adea3-006c-533e-a33c-0dfacf799aac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411624 #edb/ref 1080 "R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411624 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411624 #edb/ref 1083 #uu